d Democrats. The Republican incumbent president is term-limited, making this an open contest with no incumbency advantage for either party. Hawaii has been a reliable Democratic state in presidential voting, and nothing in the current evidence points to a fundamental change.\n\nNo public polls of the Hawaii general election, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan lean. Fundraising figures for the parties' eventual nominees have not been reported. The expected margin is roughly thirteen points for the Democrat, with wide uncertainty given the lack of race-specific data. A significant national shift toward Republicans, or unusually weak Democratic turnout, would be the most plausible path to a closer contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8784, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.877, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}]}